WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.440
Kickle Cubicle!

00:00:01.680 --> 00:00:07.440
The kingdom is covered in ice, and it's likely to stay that way unless Kickle saves the day.

00:00:22.580 --> 00:00:31.020
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:31.260 --> 00:00:33.040
I'm Mike. I'm Sean.

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:34.640
And I'm Joe.

00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:39.020
Gotta be one of the most nonsensical titles we've had on the show yet.

00:00:39.260 --> 00:00:40.540
Kickle Cubicle?

00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:42.060
It makes total sense.

00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:43.440
It's very descriptive.

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:48.260
You kick things into cubes to fall into the ground.

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:51.180
Sure. I mean, descriptive is one thing,

00:00:51.400 --> 00:00:54.320
but just call it like Kick the Cubes, right?

00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:56.500
Well, I mean, his name is Kickle, right?

00:00:56.640 --> 00:00:58.620
Because he kicks. So they named him Kickle.

00:00:58.760 --> 00:01:01.480
Because that's how naming conventions work in Kickle's world.

00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:08.100
I mean, sometimes, you know, people can be named like Sandy because they have sandy colored hair.

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:11.420
Maybe this kid was kicking on the way out and they're like, oh, let's name him Kickle.

00:01:11.620 --> 00:01:16.520
Yeah, I was gonna say, do you think that the parents ran like a genetics test with the doctor and they're like, well, he's gonna kick.

00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:18.500
So I'd suggest naming him Kickle.

00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:22.600
You don't think people ever experience babies kicking before they're born?

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:24.660
I've never heard of such a thing.

00:01:25.820 --> 00:01:27.980
Yeah, they purposely tell you not to put your...

00:01:27.980 --> 00:01:29.880
It's like the one thing you could know about a baby before they're born.

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:34.860
No, no, they tell you not to put your face too close to that woman's belly because you never know what's gonna happen.

00:01:35.180 --> 00:01:36.200
Who tells you that?

00:01:36.860 --> 00:01:38.000
Who tells you?

00:01:38.480 --> 00:01:40.000
The people in my neighborhood.

00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:43.060
They just knock on your door.

00:01:43.060 --> 00:01:45.440
They're like, hey, you better stop going up to that woman.

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:49.940
Oh, wow. Stop going up to my wife and putting your face up to her belly.

00:01:50.340 --> 00:01:52.180
Yeah. I'm like, that woman?

00:01:52.360 --> 00:01:55.200
My wife? I think I know why.

00:01:55.960 --> 00:02:06.280
Yeah. All right. Anyway, this is like another take on that genre of like Adventures of Lolo style games, puzzle games,

00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:15.220
top-down puzzle games where you have to solve things with a very specific objective through a variety,

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:23.440
like I don't even know how many, maybe 70 if not more, levels throughout this game where you play as Kickle.

00:02:24.420 --> 00:02:25.780
He's like a mime.

00:02:26.180 --> 00:02:28.520
He kind of looks like a little mime thing.

00:02:28.820 --> 00:02:34.940
Not sure exactly what he is, but maybe that's what everybody looks like in his kingdom.

00:02:34.940 --> 00:02:36.060
It's a fantasy kingdom.

00:02:36.060 --> 00:02:37.520
No, no. There's like corn people.

00:02:38.420 --> 00:02:40.180
There's like pickle people.

00:02:40.780 --> 00:02:41.980
Yeah, but those are the pickle people.

00:02:42.420 --> 00:02:44.800
Realistically proportioned human princesses.

00:02:44.960 --> 00:02:47.920
We don't know that Kickle is like a one-off.

00:02:48.040 --> 00:02:49.200
We can't say with certainty.

00:02:49.340 --> 00:02:51.760
We haven't seen everybody because everybody's been frozen.

00:02:52.280 --> 00:02:57.500
Yeah, and they're like, those princesses, they got like, it's like human proportions.

00:02:58.060 --> 00:03:02.820
So we don't know what this thing is, but we are him.

00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:09.920
Is Kickle a snowman because of all the frozenness and he like thrives in this frozen environment?

00:03:10.180 --> 00:03:19.860
I would say maybe, but like his body is too like, it's too perfect of a circle for a head, you know?

00:03:20.120 --> 00:03:21.580
There's no carrot or anything.

00:03:22.420 --> 00:03:26.080
I just kind of think it's awfully convenient what's happened in Kickle's world here.

00:03:26.320 --> 00:03:28.000
So one day-

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:30.920
Is there a problem and only I have the tools to save everyone?

00:03:31.320 --> 00:03:33.260
Yeah, but it gets even weirder than that, right?

00:03:33.320 --> 00:03:36.860
Because you could say that that's like, okay, that's what happens in Super Mario Brothers, right?

00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:40.420
Like, but anybody theoretically can jump, right?

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:41.980
Mario's just really good at it.

00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:50.180
In this universe, Kickle wakes up one day and everybody's been frozen by this, you know, wizard.

00:03:50.180 --> 00:03:55.560
And so the only person unaffected by it is Kickle.

00:03:55.840 --> 00:04:02.600
And he just happens to have the unique power of frost breath where he can turn enemies into ice.

00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:04.960
Well, isn't that what the wizard does?

00:04:05.100 --> 00:04:07.740
Like the wizard turns people into ice as well.

00:04:08.140 --> 00:04:11.360
So does the manual somewhere say that he has frost breath?

00:04:11.640 --> 00:04:12.960
It does. Yes.

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:13.780
Oh, okay.

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:15.840
Because I just assumed that-

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:19.400
Because really there's only like two enemy types that turn into ice when you kick them.

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:23.140
So I thought that was like a feature of that enemy type, not a feature of Kickle himself.

00:04:23.480 --> 00:04:26.320
And like other things sometimes kick-

00:04:26.320 --> 00:04:27.700
Well, I guess they don't turn them into ice.

00:04:27.820 --> 00:04:31.160
So never mind. But they get frozen and you can blow them up.

00:04:31.540 --> 00:04:33.320
Like you can shatter them.

00:04:33.740 --> 00:04:41.880
Yeah. It's just that a few of them will actually turn into ice cubes that you can then kick around what like a Kickle cubicle might.

00:04:42.460 --> 00:04:43.500
Yeah, I guess that's true.

00:04:43.640 --> 00:04:47.540
The things that don't turn into cubes do still freeze when you kick them.

00:04:47.800 --> 00:04:51.340
But Joe, that is an important distinction to make for those that haven't played the game.

00:04:51.500 --> 00:04:54.340
The kicking isn't what turns them into ice.

00:04:54.480 --> 00:04:55.680
It is the breath first.

00:04:55.680 --> 00:05:05.160
And then you just happen to think, well, it would be so much faster if I just kick these pieces, these ice blocks, along these other ice blocks.

00:05:05.240 --> 00:05:06.880
So this way they slide into place.

00:05:06.880 --> 00:05:13.120
It wouldn't be beneficial at all if I could just move these ice blocks individually tile by tile.

00:05:13.320 --> 00:05:15.220
No, that would take too much time.

00:05:15.640 --> 00:05:17.480
Well, I assume they're very slippery.

00:05:18.180 --> 00:05:22.200
And that would also be too easy of a puzzle solve for most of these.

00:05:23.400 --> 00:05:24.240
Right. Exactly.

00:05:24.380 --> 00:05:25.300
That's what I'm getting at, Sean.

00:05:25.620 --> 00:05:27.860
Okay. Well, you know, I didn't know that.

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:39.520
Is that, yes, basically the challenge of the game becomes that, so it's a top-down puzzle game where you basically have a set of tiles that you can walk around on in a map.

00:05:39.520 --> 00:05:52.200
But there are things that you have to collect on any given stage and enemies that you have to avoid in order to get these items.

00:05:52.400 --> 00:06:02.020
You have to then turn these enemies into ice, kick them by sliding them across the tiles that are currently available to drop them in the water.

00:06:02.020 --> 00:06:06.700
And then they create another tile that you can now walk on.

00:06:07.000 --> 00:06:13.160
If you were able to do it any other way other than sliding, you could kind of create your own path.

00:06:13.340 --> 00:06:20.300
But the challenge, of course, lies in the way that the tilesets are originally designed when you first enter a level.

00:06:20.440 --> 00:06:24.680
It's not so easy to figure out, how am I going to fill in these gaps?

00:06:24.680 --> 00:06:36.400
Sure, the beginning levels are, you know, teaching you the mechanics and it's just, well, if I just kick this block straight ahead, it's going to fill in the gap and I'll be able to walk over and collect what I need.

00:06:36.400 --> 00:06:55.580
But that's not true of later levels where you kind of have to think a little outside the box or rather try a lot of kicking and sliding in order to build something that could eventually create a way around the moats that the game has laid out for you.

00:06:55.580 --> 00:07:09.000
I was avoiding what the item was because I wasn't sure if it was balloons, which it looks like he rides between levels, or if they were just like giant red sacks like Santa Claus has for Christmas.

00:07:09.220 --> 00:07:13.920
Yeah, they just kind of look like sacks of stuff, but then there is a balloon, but that, it's just not the right image.

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:33.500
But I would say that, like, yeah, it's not so easy later, but yeah, the whole first world is pretty easy in terms of the puzzles here because they're really just teaching you, like, yeah, these are the surfaces these things can slide on.

00:07:33.760 --> 00:07:39.040
These are some enemies that can or cannot turn into cubes to then slide.

00:07:40.260 --> 00:07:55.220
But later, once it starts actually introducing new mechanics and new enemies and things like that, then it actually starts to get a little brain teaser-y, which is nice.

00:07:55.800 --> 00:08:05.760
Yeah, I consider the first world, the vegetable world, garden world, to be more about dodging the enemies.

00:08:05.760 --> 00:08:19.760
And you kind of see exactly what you got to do right away, and it's more about, yeah, getting used to dodging the enemies, learning, you know, what types of terrain the enemies can walk across is another thing that, like, you learn in the first stage.

00:08:19.940 --> 00:08:28.580
But also, yeah, the first one feels a little more about the actual quote-unquote action of it, where it's like, dodge the enemies, avoid this to get there.

00:08:29.340 --> 00:08:35.960
And I was worried it was going to stay like that, but it does then start to implement the puzzle elements.

00:08:36.120 --> 00:08:51.540
And I kind of like the way they do that because then by the time the puzzle elements really start to take off, I don't have that same complaint I've had with other games like this, where I'm like, they designed this great puzzle, but then there's all these enemies around the screen that just make me not think about puzzle.

00:08:51.540 --> 00:08:56.280
Because now I'm comfortable enough with dealing with the enemies that I can really focus on the puzzle.

00:08:56.600 --> 00:08:58.540
I don't know, I think they found a good balance with it there.

00:08:58.780 --> 00:09:10.100
Yeah, and it's not always just, you know, slide this block, you know, like freeze the enemy right where the hole is, and then just slide them into it.

00:09:10.100 --> 00:09:17.280
But it's not as elegant as that, especially in later stages, it starts to become a little bit, you have to think about, okay, well, where do I want to freeze them?

00:09:17.520 --> 00:09:24.520
And then if I slide them here, that creates like a new way for me to slide the next enemy this way, you know?

00:09:24.620 --> 00:09:31.900
So it does become a little bit of a, you have to almost visualize the solution before you fill in everything.

00:09:32.560 --> 00:09:36.340
But there's a lot of like, there's a lot of experimentation as well.

00:09:36.440 --> 00:09:41.380
There isn't necessarily like, oh, the only way to solve this puzzle is by doing this.

00:09:41.660 --> 00:09:45.440
There are a couple that are like that, but I think that there's even far between, yeah.

00:09:45.620 --> 00:09:53.540
Yeah, and what I meant was, is even more like, if you, you only have like a set amount of things that you could possibly do, and if you don't do them, you get like soft lock.

00:09:53.700 --> 00:09:59.980
Like, enemies will always just respawn, so you can just kind of like play around with the idea of like, these are the holes, how do I fill them in?

00:09:59.980 --> 00:10:04.740
If I fill in a hole that wasn't supposed to be filled in, it doesn't like, it doesn't make it impossible.

00:10:05.660 --> 00:10:19.460
No, yeah. They're well designed enough that you can have that experimentation, and you'll never end up in a situation where you are actually stuck.

00:10:19.660 --> 00:10:24.220
Was it Adventures of Lolo that has like a built-in, like, kill yourself function?

00:10:24.480 --> 00:10:25.860
Yeah, suicide button. Yeah, okay. Well, I mean, this does too.

00:10:26.020 --> 00:10:28.260
Select will automatically kill.

00:10:29.120 --> 00:10:31.660
Oh. I never pressed that button.

00:10:31.660 --> 00:10:32.540
Yeah, I discovered that by accident.

00:10:35.080 --> 00:10:37.100
But yeah, I mean, it does.

00:10:37.980 --> 00:10:39.260
Do you lose a life?

00:10:39.600 --> 00:10:40.500
I don't know.

00:10:40.900 --> 00:10:42.720
I think you do, but who cares?

00:10:42.920 --> 00:10:44.040
You have unlimited continues.

00:10:44.760 --> 00:10:53.840
So like, I mean, again, unless you care about points, but like there's a level, for example, that's one of my favorites, which is in the fruit land.

00:10:53.840 --> 00:10:58.080
And granted, I only, I'm right now in the middle of candy lands.

00:10:58.840 --> 00:11:01.520
So I did not get to the final one, which is toy land.

00:11:01.520 --> 00:11:07.780
But in fruit land, there is one where there's like a bunch of islands, and the three red bags are just sitting there.

00:11:07.860 --> 00:11:09.300
They look really easy to get to.

00:11:09.840 --> 00:11:20.800
But because of the way, this is going to be impossible to describe, but because of the way that the islands are positioned, you can only go, you can only put blocks in like straight lines from one island to another.

00:11:20.800 --> 00:11:33.880
So even though you're right next to the island you need to get to, you have to figure out like the right configuration of how to go from island to island in order to line up properly with that last island so you can make a bridge to it, if that makes any sense.

00:11:34.040 --> 00:11:44.720
But like that, coupled with the fact that like maybe, I mean, I don't know, but maybe like the very next level could be just about like avoiding enemies that are going to get like really close in on you once you open up a path for them.

00:11:44.720 --> 00:11:53.480
Like there really are a lot of different types of puzzle in this where it's like there's some that are like feel like you're up against the clock.

00:11:53.680 --> 00:11:56.820
There's some where it's like you have to really scratch your head and figure it out.

00:11:57.020 --> 00:11:59.200
Yeah, like clockwork kind of stuff.

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:08.340
I was, because that first like almost tutorial island is so long, like it's a quarter of the game.

00:12:09.520 --> 00:12:12.900
I was starting to think like, oh, this is a puzzle game for babies.

00:12:13.400 --> 00:12:23.580
It's just the, it's just that in this game, the curve, the difficulty curve is just much more gradual, I'd say.

00:12:24.240 --> 00:12:28.840
Yeah, and also like you'll be playing for a long time and think you're kind of like at the end of the game.

00:12:29.040 --> 00:12:33.860
And then like halfway through the game, they'll just drop this like crazy plot on you that like you didn't know was in there.

00:12:33.860 --> 00:12:38.600
Like it does like take a while to really get into the swing of things in this game.

00:12:38.740 --> 00:12:43.520
And I don't think it ever gets like, wow, this is like the witness style difficulty.

00:12:43.840 --> 00:12:49.900
But it's, you know, it is like I feel like a child could play this and have fun.

00:12:49.900 --> 00:12:55.440
But like, but also I played it as an adult and had fun and it did like exercise my brain a little bit.

00:12:55.560 --> 00:12:56.980
But it's also very forgiving.

00:12:57.200 --> 00:13:03.780
So like when I fail, it's like I don't have to ever start back at the beginning of the game or even of the world.

00:13:03.780 --> 00:13:05.240
I can just start on the stage I'm on.

00:13:05.360 --> 00:13:06.380
And I like that.

00:13:06.800 --> 00:13:15.120
Yeah, that's a great point, Joe, that like ultimately the lockout system of the game is just that your brain is unable to comprehend how to solve the puzzle.

00:13:15.120 --> 00:13:25.320
You know, like that, that would be the moment, not you up, you ran out of lives, got to start all the way back at the beginning tutorial levels and play for another hour and a half, two hours to get where you were.

00:13:25.500 --> 00:13:36.040
It's just more like, okay, I genuinely can't figure out how to move these mallets into the correct positions so that the ice cube hits them and it kicks it to the next area.

00:13:36.400 --> 00:13:37.720
I don't know if you guys style.

00:13:38.040 --> 00:13:40.740
Yeah, I don't know if you guys know that level, but like that was you.

00:13:40.740 --> 00:13:45.840
Yeah, that was an insane example of just kind of what Sean was talking about.

00:13:46.020 --> 00:13:54.960
The first island, even though we're calling it like a tutorial island, it's really, it's only teaching you about different setups for where to fill in blocks.

00:13:54.960 --> 00:14:02.680
It's not introducing all of the various, you know, items and enemies that you'll encounter in the other worlds.

00:14:02.680 --> 00:14:12.880
Those like springs and the hammers and the bombs, like all those things don't show up in that first world as far as I'm aware or can remember.

00:14:12.880 --> 00:14:19.060
But the, um, those mechanics are introduced as you get from like, okay, this world has this thing.

00:14:19.180 --> 00:14:31.140
And I wonder if that's just too slow of a progression for a game where, uh, it would have been better to just kind of experience every kind of small version of these things that the game has to offer.

00:14:31.140 --> 00:14:35.040
And then the puzzles just get increasingly more difficult with each world.

00:14:35.560 --> 00:14:38.060
So you're, you want less puzzles.

00:14:38.500 --> 00:14:43.980
Um, and they just like, it's more like just play the hits, right?

00:14:44.520 --> 00:14:45.620
Yes, exactly.

00:14:45.820 --> 00:14:52.960
Don't, don't make me beat this level, but then, and then take me to another level that is basically do the same exact thing.

00:14:53.060 --> 00:14:54.400
But this time it's a different shape.

00:14:55.280 --> 00:15:11.780
Yeah, I guess I just, I think that there's like, that does occur, but I think like it's, there's enough, um, there's enough variety in like the different permutations of different, of, of like different interactions, like enemy interactions.

00:15:11.820 --> 00:15:16.460
Because enemies aren't just there to chase you down, they also play with the puzzle a bit.

00:15:16.580 --> 00:15:22.320
Like there's like this bird thing that will also kick blocks if it's, if there's a block in front of it.

00:15:22.320 --> 00:15:36.400
Or, um, there's the bombs that, uh, that if you get close, they'll blow up, but they could also, uh, take out some of the area that you built, that you built a bit for a split second.

00:15:36.600 --> 00:15:45.720
Like, uh, I think there's enough like different combinations that it could feel like it's all the same puzzle, but it's actually slightly different.

00:15:45.720 --> 00:15:48.460
It just, you just know the answer already.

00:15:49.260 --> 00:16:01.940
Yeah, I, I think Mike, to your point, I, I, I was starting to feel that way from the, in the first world where I was like, okay, like I was still having a good time, but I was like, I think I get it.

00:16:02.020 --> 00:16:02.940
I think I'm good with this game.

00:16:03.060 --> 00:16:12.040
And luckily I continued playing and in the second and third world, I really think that every level in the second and third world that I played, I was like a little excited at the beginning.

00:16:12.040 --> 00:16:13.880
I was like, all right, what's this one's going to be about?

00:16:13.980 --> 00:16:17.060
And it did feel like they each kind of brought something different to the table.

00:16:17.620 --> 00:16:20.460
Um, so I, I, yeah, I do think it took them.

00:16:20.920 --> 00:16:27.540
Maybe they could be accused of taking too long to get into that point, but once they get there, I feel like it's got a, it's got a pretty good flow.

00:16:28.100 --> 00:16:39.800
Um, and also before we move on, I do want to, uh, mention there's like one core element that we are kind of missing on the gameplay in that, yes, you can kick and use your ice breath, but you can also put down like, yes.

00:16:40.880 --> 00:16:45.800
Little blockers. Pegs, like ice blockers to stop things.

00:16:46.000 --> 00:16:56.460
And that is a big core of, uh, well, one, you can like trap enemies with it, but two, that's like how you can really manipulate where your ice blocks are going to slide by like putting something for them to hit.

00:16:56.940 --> 00:17:01.300
Yeah. It took me until at least the second world to even realize that I could do that.

00:17:02.120 --> 00:17:07.360
Yeah. I realized I could do it in the first row, but it took me until the second world to like understand the utility of it.

00:17:07.440 --> 00:17:08.880
I was like, well, why would I ever need this?

00:17:08.960 --> 00:17:13.440
I can just kind of kick these things wherever I want and just freeze the things where I want them to be.

00:17:13.580 --> 00:17:20.060
But yeah, once the puzzles get more complicated, you do start to need to manipulate the landscape yourself a little more.

00:17:44.420 --> 00:17:50.980
It's funny because it sounds like we're almost saying, well, if they just started this game on the second world, like, uh, we'd have almost no complaints.

00:17:50.980 --> 00:17:55.240
Cause you would just get right into the variety and yeah, you'd, it'd be hard, but you'd learn by trial.

00:17:55.380 --> 00:18:01.860
It'd be like saying, you know, Hey, super Mario brothers was great, but they didn't introduce pitfalls until world two one.

00:18:02.100 --> 00:18:04.860
And I wish they would have just put pitfalls in the first stage.

00:18:05.480 --> 00:18:20.460
Yeah. I mean, I, I think I'm saying it, if, if anything, I'm saying that maybe the first world should have been shorter, but I didn't mind the, uh, the setup of, um, like a little bit of a setup of like, these are the easy levels.

00:18:20.460 --> 00:18:21.300
Get you acquainted.

00:18:21.880 --> 00:18:27.920
And, and I played this, uh, at first I was, we were alternating me and my girlfriend who does not play video games like at all.

00:18:28.180 --> 00:18:35.540
So like, I think for her particularly, it was helpful to get those, like, just to get the feel of like a controller, let alone this game.

00:18:35.720 --> 00:18:43.600
And I, and I'm assuming, you know, in 1990, there were probably a lot more people that weren't as, you know, versed in video games that would be buying this.

00:18:43.600 --> 00:18:52.940
Or if it was targeted towards people who like, this is one of their first video games or something, um, you know, really give them a good runway to, to get used to it.

00:18:53.860 --> 00:18:54.840
Yeah. It's funny you say that.

00:18:54.920 --> 00:19:04.280
Cause it doesn't, it does feel like a, we mentioned it before and, but we also had the caveat that like, well, but this was a brain teaser for me.

00:19:04.280 --> 00:19:09.240
It does feel like it is more aimed at kids than most puzzle games.

00:19:09.760 --> 00:19:13.440
Uh, so I, I, I agree with that.

00:19:14.440 --> 00:19:20.720
And that's probably because of the story, uh, the graphical presentation and everything.

00:19:20.920 --> 00:19:26.280
But I will say though, that the, uh, the talking corn on the cob is a little much for a child.

00:19:26.440 --> 00:19:28.260
I think that that should have been censored.

00:19:29.520 --> 00:19:41.140
Understood. Uh, I, I was just gonna say that the graphical presentation of the game though, while it might look a bit kiddish because of Kickle and the other, uh, other characters, uh, it's a beautiful looking game.

00:19:41.920 --> 00:19:43.560
Absolutely. Yeah, it's clean.

00:19:43.940 --> 00:19:48.360
And, uh, a couple of other things about just the moment to moment gameplay.

00:19:48.700 --> 00:19:53.060
There are, um, there are time limits for the levels.

00:19:53.200 --> 00:19:56.600
And when the timer runs out, you don't just automatically die.

00:19:56.780 --> 00:20:04.080
An enemy comes on the screen and, you know, basically just targeting you and will kill you.

00:20:04.160 --> 00:20:09.080
But it's possible to like finish the stage while that's still happening.

00:20:09.080 --> 00:20:15.100
Like you do, you do have the ability to dodge that enemy, but you just can't do it like for infinity.

00:20:15.280 --> 00:20:17.360
Like I think eventually it would catch up to you.

00:20:18.280 --> 00:20:29.000
Yeah. It's kind of reminds me of like the ghost and Spelunky or just those like hurry up things, uh, where it's a bit more forgiving, where it's not like you're about to touch.

00:20:29.000 --> 00:20:33.680
And then you just watch that last sack of goodies or whatever those things are supposed to be.

00:20:34.040 --> 00:20:38.100
And suddenly the game just stops and you drop dead like a Mario.

00:20:38.680 --> 00:20:48.840
Um, I, I, I like the, the little bit of, again, uh, the word we were using was, uh, what was the word you were using, Joe?

00:20:50.500 --> 00:20:53.060
Uh, forgiveness, forgive, forgiveness.

00:20:54.220 --> 00:20:55.080
It's like a context.

00:20:55.820 --> 00:20:57.240
It makes sense though, too, right?

00:20:57.360 --> 00:21:01.620
Like the timer goes out and your character just dies in Mario and Sonic.

00:21:01.840 --> 00:21:05.360
But why? Why do they just die when the timer goes out?

00:21:05.420 --> 00:21:06.440
Because video games. Yeah.

00:21:06.560 --> 00:21:14.820
But like, did, did they have like, did Bowser shoot like a homing missile at Mario that was like, if it, if it takes him 350 seconds, we'll be able to hit him.

00:21:14.960 --> 00:21:16.000
You were like canonically, why do they die?

00:21:16.220 --> 00:21:19.200
Yeah. Right. You know, at least in this one, it's like, oh yeah, it makes sense.

00:21:19.300 --> 00:21:23.340
Like time's up and now this enemy has arrived that, uh, you can't defeat.

00:21:23.680 --> 00:21:27.520
I like it. Yeah. I think in Mario it was supposed to be like, it was supposed to be like, he's aging.

00:21:28.360 --> 00:21:30.220
And if you wait too long, you're just going to die.

00:21:30.220 --> 00:21:33.240
Like in real life, the longer you live, the more likely you are to die.

00:21:33.480 --> 00:21:34.300
Oh, I don't like that.

00:21:34.420 --> 00:21:36.940
I don't, I don't like that, uh, interpretation.

00:21:38.780 --> 00:21:44.060
And speaking of death in this game, uh, you guys both mentioned that it's forgiving and that you're able to just continue.

00:21:44.060 --> 00:21:48.860
But the manual states something a little odd about, uh, the death in this game.

00:21:48.980 --> 00:21:53.600
It says you have two options after you lose your last kickle.

00:21:53.820 --> 00:21:58.420
Kickle is taking the place of what we usually refer to in video games as lives.

00:21:58.420 --> 00:22:01.400
Are they implying that there are multiple kickles?

00:22:01.680 --> 00:22:02.780
Your last kickle?

00:22:03.680 --> 00:22:09.600
Yeah, because you've heard this before with like a new, you get a one man or like a new man or whatever.

00:22:09.900 --> 00:22:12.460
Like, yeah, they just haven't codified.

00:22:13.480 --> 00:22:14.500
Maybe, I don't know.

00:22:14.640 --> 00:22:17.860
It just sounds like they don't know to use the term lives.

00:22:18.220 --> 00:22:19.100
Yes, I agree.

00:22:19.420 --> 00:22:20.420
I think it's like a multiverse thing.

00:22:20.960 --> 00:22:23.680
Right, that's what I was trying to get to that point.

00:22:23.840 --> 00:22:31.020
I like that better, but I just think it's funny to point out when the manual says things that are like, huh, we have solutions for that now, but we didn't back then.

00:22:31.020 --> 00:22:40.720
There are boss fights in this game too, uh, as odd as that sounds, but, uh, that's the one time where like, you know, it's a little different from the puzzle mechanics.

00:22:40.980 --> 00:22:43.440
It's the end of each world, so there's not too many bosses.

00:22:43.640 --> 00:22:45.740
There's not any like sub bosses or anything like that.

00:22:45.900 --> 00:22:48.800
Uh, the manual descriptions for these guys are also good.

00:22:48.940 --> 00:22:57.180
Uh, for the Garden Land boss, Coke, uh, he's a, the, uh, the big bird is bad and he's after you.

00:22:57.440 --> 00:23:04.480
Be careful. Uh, Pyro in Fruitland, he is the lord of Fruitland now.

00:23:05.340 --> 00:23:08.280
Okay. So who was before?

00:23:08.560 --> 00:23:09.680
It was the princess.

00:23:10.480 --> 00:23:11.380
Of Fruitland?

00:23:11.540 --> 00:23:14.060
Oh yeah, that's true. The curvaceous princess.

00:23:14.780 --> 00:23:18.080
The way you, the way you said now gives it like a different context.

00:23:18.240 --> 00:23:19.780
Like, he's the lord of Fruitland now.

00:23:20.000 --> 00:23:21.020
Yeah, yeah, exactly.

00:23:21.020 --> 00:23:23.880
Like, I don't, it's not, I'm assuming that's not the way it was read.

00:23:24.040 --> 00:23:25.700
Like, you don't, you don't want to know what he was before.

00:23:28.100 --> 00:23:32.980
Um, and the wicked wizard king, the master of evil himself.

00:23:32.980 --> 00:23:36.480
Now that seems, you know, a little bit like hyperbole.

00:23:36.620 --> 00:23:37.800
The master of evil.

00:23:37.880 --> 00:23:39.640
All he did was like freeze everybody.

00:23:39.800 --> 00:23:40.840
You can unfreeze them.

00:23:41.680 --> 00:23:45.080
What would you think of someone that froze everybody?

00:23:45.800 --> 00:23:47.040
Like, that they're good guys?

00:23:47.440 --> 00:23:49.720
I don't know if they would be considered the master of evil.

00:23:49.840 --> 00:23:51.580
They didn't open the gate to hell.

00:23:52.080 --> 00:23:52.980
Sure, sure.

00:23:53.080 --> 00:23:55.280
But like, that's still like not a nice thing to do.

00:23:55.900 --> 00:24:01.440
Yes. So, anybody who wrongs you in your day to day, you can, you can call them the master of evil.

00:24:01.600 --> 00:24:08.680
But I don't know if, I don't know if freezing four entire countries is like equivalent to like wronging you in your day to day.

00:24:09.040 --> 00:24:13.780
Listen, Kickel is unaffected by the changes, so I don't even understand why he cares.

00:24:14.020 --> 00:24:19.100
Look, I think we're, we're, we're kind of, you were kind of getting at in the very top of the show.

00:24:19.620 --> 00:24:21.000
I think he did it.

00:24:21.280 --> 00:24:22.320
That's what I'm saying.

00:24:22.600 --> 00:24:24.340
Kickel has no stakes in the game.

00:24:24.340 --> 00:24:26.160
The world is now better off for him.

00:24:26.360 --> 00:24:27.160
It's all frozen.

00:24:27.460 --> 00:24:29.080
He enjoys. He's like the hero now.

00:24:29.680 --> 00:24:31.720
Exactly. Yeah, I agree.

00:24:32.320 --> 00:24:35.500
He's the Captain Quark of, uh, of the Kickel universe.

00:24:36.220 --> 00:24:37.260
Someone will get that.

00:24:37.520 --> 00:24:40.220
Who's that? He's from Ratchet and Clank.

00:24:40.400 --> 00:24:42.360
Ah. Spoiler for Ratchet and Clank 1.

00:24:44.560 --> 00:24:50.300
There are, uh, not sequels and spinoffs to this game, but there are regional differences between them.

00:24:50.380 --> 00:24:51.300
And I thought this was interesting.

00:24:51.300 --> 00:24:54.260
The Japanese version apparently has more enemies.

00:24:55.500 --> 00:24:56.840
I don't really know if it needed it.

00:24:57.360 --> 00:24:58.460
Yeah. I think that the.

00:24:58.740 --> 00:25:06.280
I guess it depends on what the enemies are and if they have some kind of specific, uh, like, gameplay element.

00:25:06.660 --> 00:25:12.120
Sure, but like, I guess I mean, like, aren't the, aren't the levels, like, designed with these enemies in mind?

00:25:12.260 --> 00:25:14.140
Like, so now you're just putting more of them on the screen?

00:25:14.400 --> 00:25:16.020
Well, were there more levels too?

00:25:16.020 --> 00:25:22.060
Uh, it does not say that, but it does say something really interesting about the levels.

00:25:22.260 --> 00:25:23.120
I don't want to hear that.

00:25:23.480 --> 00:25:31.900
Yeah. So, what I'll tell you is, the Japanese version, you don't, you get to choose which land you want to go to first.

00:25:32.500 --> 00:25:36.600
Oh. So, you can go to garden land, fruit land, toy land, any order.

00:25:37.300 --> 00:25:43.140
See, that seems, that seems very strange because they clearly seem to have a level of progression to them.

00:25:43.320 --> 00:25:46.460
They just trusted the Japanese to make better decisions.

00:25:47.380 --> 00:26:02.140
But I mean, if you, like, imagine you start on, I don't know, like, cake land and you end up, like, your last one is garden land, which is level three and level one respectively, I believe.

00:26:02.400 --> 00:26:05.960
Like, isn't that just going to be, like, a boring time?

00:26:06.700 --> 00:26:08.320
Yeah, that's what I'm saying.

00:26:08.320 --> 00:26:15.740
But also, maybe they were, like, yeah, those Americans are all going to go to cake land immediately if we don't make them start somewhere else.

00:26:16.120 --> 00:26:21.640
And I mentioned that this is a Adventures of Lolo style game.

00:26:21.900 --> 00:26:25.200
Did you guys make that comparison at all?

00:26:25.280 --> 00:26:26.940
How do you feel about that comparison?

00:26:27.520 --> 00:26:29.620
Were you keeping that in mind as you were playing?

00:26:29.940 --> 00:26:33.520
Do you have a preference? I absolutely made that comparison.

00:26:33.660 --> 00:26:36.080
I feel like it's hard to not make that comparison.

00:26:36.320 --> 00:26:37.780
Because they are two separate companies.

00:26:38.780 --> 00:26:40.340
Yeah. Yeah, weird.

00:26:40.540 --> 00:26:47.040
I blame Mike incepting that into our brains because he did say it was like Adventures of Lolo.

00:26:47.560 --> 00:26:54.040
Yeah, but I mean, would you not, like, would you not look at this immediately and say he kind of looks like Lolo?

00:26:54.060 --> 00:26:59.640
Well, now I'll never know because I didn't get to have that opinion on my own.

00:27:00.200 --> 00:27:04.060
I don't remember Mike saying this before, so maybe I was incepted.

00:27:04.060 --> 00:27:05.360
But, I mean, he looks like Lolo.

00:27:05.360 --> 00:27:09.160
He's doing the same thing kind of as Lolo walking on this top-down thing.

00:27:09.980 --> 00:27:13.180
It's true. I just, just giving him a hard time.

00:27:14.560 --> 00:27:16.040
Well, I mean, which you should do.

00:27:16.220 --> 00:27:17.820
I'm not, I'm not discouraging that.

00:27:17.820 --> 00:27:23.100
But, um, but I think that, uh, what was your question?

00:27:23.380 --> 00:27:24.440
Would I prefer one?

00:27:24.580 --> 00:27:28.240
No, I actually asked, like, eight questions to confuse you, to make you say, what's your question?

00:27:28.620 --> 00:27:33.460
Is Adventures of Lolo an apt comparison?

00:27:34.000 --> 00:27:35.420
And is that bad?

00:27:35.860 --> 00:27:37.060
Yes, that's a great question.

00:27:37.700 --> 00:27:38.920
No, that's what you asked.

00:27:39.200 --> 00:27:40.500
Yeah, yeah, that's why it's a great question.

00:27:40.660 --> 00:27:41.800
I thought you also asked, like, do you have a preference?

00:27:41.800 --> 00:27:46.140
I did say, I did sneak that in there, but then I was hoping you would just ignore it.

00:27:46.360 --> 00:27:47.600
I'll ignore that for now.

00:27:47.960 --> 00:27:52.820
My preference is for nothing to be like games I've played before, ever.

00:28:12.920 --> 00:28:20.300
But, so like I said before, this game doles out, like, the plot very, um, conservatively.

00:28:21.580 --> 00:28:24.020
And it keeps surprising me with, like, more stuff.

00:28:24.180 --> 00:28:29.380
You know, like, first of all, you beat the first level, you save a bunch of carrots, and then you move on to the second level.

00:28:29.620 --> 00:28:30.920
And you're like, okay, this is the game.

00:28:31.080 --> 00:28:36.140
Then you save a bunch of corn, and then one of the pieces of, one of the ears of corn is like, here's all the lore.

00:28:36.760 --> 00:28:38.420
But the carrots didn't say anything.

00:28:38.460 --> 00:28:39.860
That's what I'm saying, that corn guy.

00:28:41.000 --> 00:28:50.380
Yeah, yeah. And then that happens again in the fruit world, where it's like, you're like six levels in, and all of a sudden, like, an orange starts talking to you.

00:28:50.460 --> 00:28:54.240
But nobody was telling you what you're doing in fruit world before that.

00:28:54.300 --> 00:28:58.280
And then they're like, oh, hey, glad you've been doing this because our princess has been kidnapped or whatever.

00:28:58.960 --> 00:29:00.740
So, so I'm like, okay, this is going to be it.

00:29:00.900 --> 00:29:06.880
But, but like, you get to that first boss, and you've already been playing the game for a while, because there's a lot of levels in each world.

00:29:07.320 --> 00:29:09.620
So then I'm like, oh, there are boss battles in this?

00:29:09.620 --> 00:29:10.820
I thought that was the end of the game.

00:29:11.460 --> 00:29:13.440
And then it's like, okay, now there's another world.

00:29:13.660 --> 00:29:21.980
And then you get to like the, to the end, and this is a really long, and I watched this, I didn't get to, I didn't get through the fourth world.

00:29:21.980 --> 00:29:23.100
But I watched this on YouTube.

00:29:24.000 --> 00:29:30.900
Very long, like, cut scene about, like, the fantasy kingdom, and like the, you know, you've restored it, and the king is there, and all this stuff.

00:29:31.040 --> 00:29:34.980
And then it, the credits, and then it's like, and now enjoy this new game.

00:29:35.640 --> 00:29:36.700
This special game.

00:29:36.840 --> 00:29:40.240
And there's like all these special, like, more, you know, challenging levels.

00:29:40.240 --> 00:29:47.260
It's like a whole, and in the YouTube video, that's like, it's like a quarter of the YouTube video, if not more, is just the post game.

00:29:47.740 --> 00:29:49.200
Yeah, like challenge levels.

00:29:49.960 --> 00:29:52.760
Yeah. And they're really hard levels, that's why.

00:29:52.880 --> 00:29:56.280
They're like long, complicated levels, and I think there's 30 of them.

00:29:56.480 --> 00:30:10.920
I guess it's like, you know, I know that there's some, like, story modes of, you know, not, not very story forward games that are like, think of it like, like Call of Duty.

00:30:10.920 --> 00:30:22.360
Like, there's a, there's a campaign, but the campaign doesn't really matter, so it's gonna be pretty short, and all of, like, the high level play is in the not story mode.

00:30:22.520 --> 00:30:24.000
So that, that's how I'm looking at it.

00:30:24.440 --> 00:30:26.200
I mean, that's kind of what it feels like.

00:30:26.300 --> 00:30:27.780
Do we know, do this have a battery?

00:30:28.140 --> 00:30:29.400
Could you save this game?

00:30:29.740 --> 00:30:32.420
I think it's password function. Okay, well then that's good, yeah.

00:30:32.520 --> 00:30:39.340
So like, yeah, you get the whole game out of the way, and then like the rest of your enjoyment is like, let's try and solve another puzzle in Kickle Cubicle today.

00:30:39.340 --> 00:30:45.340
And we have an opportunity to tackle another game on the Essential Games list.

00:30:48.560 --> 00:30:54.800
Kickle Cubicle is pretty darn good and fun game.

00:30:55.220 --> 00:31:01.260
So why am I starting with that instead of telling you how I really feel about it?

00:31:01.260 --> 00:31:09.840
Well, because I think Kickle Cubicle is like perfectly serviceable for what it sets out to do.

00:31:10.100 --> 00:31:18.920
And so if it does everything that it wanted, and it does it well, and it controls great, why shouldn't I put it on the Essential Games list, right?

00:31:18.920 --> 00:31:29.540
Like, it meets every checkbox for like what makes a good video game, except for the personal part of it.

00:31:29.680 --> 00:31:31.660
Like the part where it's like, do you like the game?

00:31:31.760 --> 00:31:33.400
And I don't know if I would check that box.

00:31:33.600 --> 00:31:41.040
I don't know if I personally enjoyed sliding these ice blocks into holes, which is like the entire point of the game.

00:31:41.040 --> 00:31:50.120
Like, I don't know if I like that kind of puzzle, which is weird, because I liked what Lolo was putting down for puzzles, essentially just finding creative ways to kill all the enemies on screen.

00:31:50.120 --> 00:32:03.100
And so have I been trained by video games to just be focused on killing things and that being a good enough reward, but building land structures is not rewarding enough for me?

00:32:03.940 --> 00:32:10.200
It's weird, but I don't think in good faith I can put this on the Essential Games list if I personally wasn't loving it.

00:32:10.280 --> 00:32:17.400
But I think it needs to be mentioned that if this game at all sounded fun to you, it is actually fun.

00:32:17.400 --> 00:32:18.240
It is something.

00:32:18.620 --> 00:32:20.940
These puzzles do get more challenging.

00:32:20.940 --> 00:32:24.840
They get very creative. I wouldn't just label this a kid's game by any means.

00:32:25.000 --> 00:32:38.640
I do think that it's very impressive to see somebody else kind of tackle this Lolo genre, which we've already said is an essential game, and just kind of nail it doing a completely different thing.

00:32:39.320 --> 00:32:43.860
Sean? Yeah, I think this is a super charming game.

00:32:45.200 --> 00:32:50.520
I think it gets off to a slow start in terms of how puzzly it feels.

00:32:53.060 --> 00:32:57.300
But it's got everything a puzzle game should have.

00:32:58.100 --> 00:33:04.200
It's just the pacing is a little slower than you're used to.

00:33:04.200 --> 00:33:09.800
So I'm kind of at a crossroads here because I think I do enjoy this better than Lolo.

00:33:11.220 --> 00:33:17.420
And I think I voted for it, but that could or could not be the case.

00:33:18.880 --> 00:33:22.720
So now I'm thinking, like, does that mean that Lolo's not essential?

00:33:23.160 --> 00:33:24.840
Does that mean that this is?

00:33:25.940 --> 00:33:31.100
And for me, at least, I'm going to err on the side of positivity.

00:33:32.420 --> 00:33:33.440
And you know what?

00:33:33.640 --> 00:33:40.040
Maybe it doesn't deserve to be on there, but I'm going to vote for it to be an essential game.

00:33:40.360 --> 00:33:41.840
All right. One vote.

00:33:42.180 --> 00:33:43.240
Joe, what are you doing?

00:33:43.240 --> 00:33:45.440
I had a little bit of a journey with this, too.

00:33:45.500 --> 00:33:52.860
And I noticed that I do often take you through my whole journey of where I landed on these

00:33:52.860 --> 00:33:53.960
for essential games list, folks.

00:33:55.280 --> 00:33:59.820
But I did start with this thinking, like, yeah, this game's not doing anything wrong.

00:34:00.440 --> 00:34:04.960
But, like, something about it just didn't feel essential game level.

00:34:05.220 --> 00:34:07.120
And I was struggling with that for a while.

00:34:07.700 --> 00:34:12.860
But then I did get to, yeah, some, like, World 2 and World 3.

00:34:13.040 --> 00:34:18.560
Whereas in World 1, I'm, like, trying to decide why, what's a reason not to put this on the

00:34:18.560 --> 00:34:22.660
essential games list? Because I really was thinking it was good, but it just didn't feel enough.

00:34:23.260 --> 00:34:26.360
But then by the next two worlds, I was really liking it.

00:34:26.460 --> 00:34:29.420
And, yeah, I'll also answer your question from earlier about preference.

00:34:29.480 --> 00:34:31.380
It's been a while since I played Adventure of Lolo.

00:34:31.380 --> 00:34:37.740
But I think if you asked me between the two of them right now, I'm definitely voting for

00:34:37.740 --> 00:34:40.260
Kickle Cubicle over Adventures of Lolo.

00:34:41.400 --> 00:34:42.980
So I'd have to replay that one.

00:34:43.080 --> 00:34:45.040
But, yeah, right now my preference is Kickle Cubicle.

00:34:45.320 --> 00:34:46.360
I had a lot of fun.

00:34:46.400 --> 00:34:49.720
I really had fun pushing these ice blocks into the water.

00:34:50.320 --> 00:34:56.500
And I found a lot of satisfaction from actually solving each of these puzzles.

00:34:58.740 --> 00:35:06.280
And especially just the fact that there's, like, a lot of great game design and charm in this game

00:35:06.280 --> 00:35:09.340
on top of pretty good puzzle design.

00:35:10.100 --> 00:35:13.600
The perfect level of, like, forgiving gameplay.

00:35:14.000 --> 00:35:15.420
I mean, I really like this game.

00:35:15.420 --> 00:35:21.380
And I have no more doubts about whether to put it on.

00:35:21.480 --> 00:35:22.920
I want to put it on the essential games list.

00:35:22.920 --> 00:35:27.300
And I don't think I realized, you know, so obviously I knew I was voting it on.

00:35:27.540 --> 00:35:31.660
But it wasn't until, Mike, you voted no and, Sean, I was curious if you were voting or not

00:35:31.660 --> 00:35:34.840
that I was like, wow, I'm going to be really disappointed if this doesn't go on.

00:35:35.100 --> 00:35:39.480
Because I think I was originally coming on and about to say, like, yeah, I'm going to vote this on.

00:35:39.680 --> 00:35:43.120
But, you know, I could see maybe in the future it coming off.

